 "fieldData": {
  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Background Check Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a background check form?\", \"a\": \"It is the document an applicant or employee signs so an employer can order a background report. Under FTC guidance it pairs a disclosure that stands alone with written permission to run the check.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you create a background check form?\", \"a\": \"Put the disclosure on a screen by itself, follow it with the signature page, and collect only the details your screening provider asks for. Describe that to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a> and it builds the form, the signature step, and a tracker for each candidate as one app.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a background check authorization form include?\", \"a\": \"A complete packet has three parts. The disclosure says a report may be ordered for employment purposes. The authorization asks for a signature and date. A short details section gives the provider what it needs to find the right records, such as former names and past addresses. Describe those parts to Emergent and it builds each one as a separate screen.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can you include a background check consent form in a job application?\", \"a\": \"No. FTC guidance says the disclosure has to be a document of its own. It cannot sit inside the <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/job-application-form'>job application form</a>, and a consent checkbox at the bottom of the application does not count. Send it as a separate screen or document.\"}, {\"q\": \"Do you send an employee background check form to current staff?\", \"a\": \"Yes, when the employer orders a report from a screening company about someone already on the payroll. FTC guidance asks for written permission from applicants and employees alike. Keep that disclosure in its own document, apart from the new employee form packet.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can you collect a background check consent form online?\", \"a\": \"Yes. The federal E-SIGN Act lets electronic signatures and records stand in for paper ones. Where a law says a consumer must get information in writing, the person first has to agree to receive it electronically. Put that agreement before the disclosure; Emergent can build it as the opening screen of the form.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can you use one background check authorization form for checks after hire?\", \"a\": \"It can, if the disclosure says so plainly. In a 1998 advisory opinion, FTC staff wrote that one disclosure and one permission can cover reports during the application and throughout employment, as long as the disclosure clearly states that scope. Staff opinions are informal. Have counsel confirm the wording.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the retention period for a background check authorization form?\", \"a\": \"At least one year where EEOC recordkeeping rules apply. They require personnel and employment records, hiring records among them, to be kept for a year from the date the record was made or the action taken, whichever is later. With a form built in Emergent, each signed copy is a row in your own database, and you choose the day it gets deleted.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the next step if a candidate refuses to sign a background check consent form?\", \"a\": \"Then the employer cannot order the report. Written permission has to come first, and the employer certifies to the screening company that it has it. What happens to the application after a refusal is a policy decision. Set it with counsel and apply it to every candidate the same way.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the difference between a background check consent and authorization form?\", \"a\": \"In practice, none. Both names describe the page where the applicant gives written permission for the report. Under FTC guidance the label matters less than two things: a disclosure on its own document, and a signature collected before anything is ordered.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
}